\title{hyperutils: Fast Exploratory Analysis, Non-Compartmental Analysis and Custom Outputs for Clinical Pharmacology}
\description{
A toolbox for the day-to-day data work of clinical pharmacology and
pharmacometrics: read NONMEM-style datasets, explore them with
publication-quality plots and tables, run non-compartmental analysis (NCA) of
every profile, and build custom ggplot2 figures and summary tables, with the
numerical core written in C++ ('Rcpp') for speed.
}
\section{What the package does}{

\itemize{
\item \strong{Read and prepare data.} \code{\link[=hp_read_data]{hp_read_data()}} reads comma, tab,
semicolon or white-space delimited files and NONMEM table files;
\code{\link[=hp_mutate]{hp_mutate()}}, \code{\link[=hp_filter]{hp_filter()}} and \code{\link[=hp_case_when]{hp_case_when()}} run data steps written as
R expressions; \code{\link[=hp_custom_prep]{hp_custom_prep()}} chains mutate, filter, distinct, sort,
head and select.
\item \strong{Explore.} \code{\link[=hp_eda_prepare]{hp_eda_prepare()}} derives time after dose, dose-normalised
concentrations, BLQ flags and observed exposure per subject.
\code{\link[=hp_eda_plot]{hp_eda_plot()}} draws concentration-time, covariate, distribution, summary
and correlation figures from a specification built with \code{\link[=hp_eda_spec]{hp_eda_spec()}};
\code{\link[=hp_eda_table]{hp_eda_table()}} and friends build descriptive tables.
\item \strong{Non-compartmental analysis.} \code{\link[=hp_nca]{hp_nca()}} analyses every dosing
interval of every subject (oral, intravenous bolus and infusion, single
dose and steady state) with WinNonlin-style rules for areas, BLQ data and
the terminal slope, and returns parameter, summary, terminal-slope and
profile tables. \code{\link[=hp_nca_plots]{hp_nca_plots()}} draws the standard NCA figures.
\item \strong{Custom outputs.} \code{\link[=hp_custom_plot]{hp_custom_plot()}} and \code{\link[=hp_custom_table]{hp_custom_table()}} turn a
plain list describing a plot or a table into a ggplot2 figure or a
summary table (demographics, frequency, pivot, correlation).
\item \strong{Fast.} Areas, partial areas, moments, terminal-phase regressions and
exposure measures are computed in C++ (\code{\link[=hp_curve_area]{hp_curve_area()}},
\code{\link[=hp_lz_windows]{hp_lz_windows()}}, \code{\link[=hp_exposure]{hp_exposure()}} and others), which you can also call
directly.
}
